 y0), then"},{"x":0.39286,"y":0.57392,"w":0.21505,"h":0.06906,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"adƒ"},{"x":0.59245,"y":0.57392,"w":0.02439,"h":0.06906,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dsb"},{"x":0.64826,"y":0.59156,"w":0.273,"h":0.03333,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"= (∇ƒ)P0 # u,\b (4)"},{"x":0.61849,"y":0.61631,"w":0.02242,"h":0.01116,"fs":0.0087,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"u, P0"},{"x":0.39286,"y":0.62481,"w":0.46338,"h":0.02904,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"the dot product of the gradient ∇ƒ at P0 and u. In brief, Du ƒ = ∇ƒ # u."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.6753,"w":0.59305,"h":0.03705,"fs":0.01407,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Example 2 Find the derivative of ƒ(x, y) = xey + cos (xy) at the point (2, 0) in the direction of v = 3i - 4j."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.72543,"w":0.59297,"h":0.03063,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution Recall that the direction of a vector v is the unit vector obtained by dividing v by its length:"},{"x":0.57016,"y":0.75729,"w":0.17378,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":6,"k":0,"t":"u = v 0 v0 = v 5 = 3 5 i - 4 5 j."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.81469,"w":0.52284,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The partial derivatives of ƒ are everywhere continuous and at (2, 0) are given by"},{"x":0.48032,"y":0.83644,"w":0.29962,"h":0.02426,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"fx(2, 0) = (ey - y sin (xy))(2,0) = e0 - 0 = 1"},{"x":0.48032,"y":0.85341,"w":0.33815,"h":0.02904,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"fy(2, 0) = (xey - x sin (xy))(2,0) = 2e0 - 2 # 0 = 2."},{"x":0.52275,"y":0.88454,"w":0.26942,"h":0.07735,"fs":0.01353,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"∇ƒ0 (2,0) = ƒx(2, 0)i + ƒy(2, 0)j = i + 2j"},{"x":0.36257,"y":0.89014,"w":0.18355,"h":0.01427,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"The gradient of ƒ at (2, 0) is"}]}